1. Build the ICMP packet yourself
With SOCK_RAW + IPPROTO_ICMP, the kernel builds the IP header for you automatically (unless you use IP_HDRINCL), but you must build the full ICMP message in a buffer:

ICMP header (struct icmp or struct icmphdr from <netinet/ip_icmp.h>):
type = ICMP_ECHO (8) for an echo request
code = 0
id = usually your process PID, so you can match replies to your own process if multiple pings run
sequence = an incrementing counter, one per packet sent
checksum = computed last, over the whole ICMP message (header + payload), with the checksum field itself set to 0 before computing
Payload (optional): arbitrary data, often used to carry a timestamp (e.g. result of gettimeofday) so that when the reply comes back you can compute round-trip time. Real ping fills this with a pattern of bytes.
2. Compute the checksum
ICMP requires the classic Internet checksum algorithm: sum all 16-bit words of the ICMP header+payload, fold carries back into 16 bits, then take the one's complement. This is a standalone function you write once and reuse for every packet.

3. Send the packet
Use sendto() (not send(), since raw ICMP sockets aren't "connected" by default) with:

the socket fd
pointer to your ICMP buffer and its length
a struct sockaddr_in destination filled with the target's IP (from resolving the hostname/IP argument, e.g. via getaddrinfo) and sin_family = AF_INET (no port needed for ICMP, ICMP doesn't use ports)
4. Receive the reply
Use recvfrom() on the same socket. The kernel delivers you the full IP packet (IP header + ICMP header + payload) since it's a raw socket, even though you only sent the ICMP part. So you must:

skip past the IP header (its length is variable — read the IHL field, low 4 bits of the first byte, multiply by 4 to get header length in bytes)
parse the ICMP header that follows: check type is ICMP_ECHOREPLY (0), and that id/sequence match what you sent (since raw sockets can receive ICMP traffic not meant for you)
5. Timing and loop
Record send timestamp before sendto(), compute RTT when the matching reply arrives.
Typically loop with a 1-second interval (sleep/nanosleep/setitimer + alarm), incrementing sequence each iteration, until interrupted (e.g. SIGINT via Ctrl+C) — then print summary statistics (packets sent/received, min/avg/max RTT).
Often a recvfrom() timeout is set (via setsockopt with SO_RCVTIMEO) so you can detect and report packet loss ("Request timeout").
That's the full round trip: build → checksum → sendto → recvfrom → parse/validate → measure RTT → repeat. Let me know if you want to go deeper into any single step (e.g. the checksum algorithm, or the ICMP/IP header struct layouts).